# Bootstrapping a new iOS project for agentic CLI delivery

This rule is for the **brand-new-project** case. If the user is joining an
already-configured project (just needs credentials + machine setup), point
them at `.cursor/rules/ios-project-onboarding.mdc` instead.

The end state after this walkthrough: the user's new project has a working
Fastlane + Firebase App Distribution + TestFlight delivery pipeline that an
agent can drive via `fastlane ios <lane>` one-liners.

## What you're signing up for (state this up front)

Tell the user, before starting:

> Realistic timeline: this is **~1 full afternoon of hands-on work**, plus
> potentially **24–48 hours of waiting** for Apple's first TestFlight build
> review. There's a **$99/year Apple Developer Program fee**. If you're doing
> this for a client, get the client's Apple ID on the Developer account or
> you'll own the signing identity forever.
>
> Most of this is irreversible once it's live (bundle ID, team association,
> App Store listing), so I'll flag the gates as we go.

## Operating principles for this walkthrough

- **Sequential, not parallel.** Apple Developer Program must exist before
  provisioning profiles. Profiles must exist before Fastlane can sign.
  Fastlane must be configured before distribution works.
- **Confirm at every gate.** Before each "create a new thing in a Console"
  step, recap what's about to happen and confirm.
- **Flag costs.** $99/year Developer fee, potential $299/year for
  organizations (requires DUNS number).
- **Save credentials immediately.** Every cert/key/profile you generate,
  immediately tell the user to back it up (1Password, team vault).
- **Distinguish what's per-account from what's per-app.** The Apple Developer
  account is per-organization (or per-individual). App IDs, profiles, and
  App Store listings are per-app.

## Prerequisites (Step 0)

Before touching any console, confirm with the user:

- They're on **macOS** (hard requirement)
- They have an **Xcode project that builds locally** (`xcodebuild build`
  succeeds for at least one scheme). If not — they need basic project setup
  first, which is outside this rule's scope.
- They have a **bundle identifier chosen** and set in their Xcode project
  (e.g., `com.company.appname`). This is permanent once published.
- They have an **Apple ID** they're willing to associate with the Developer
  account.
- They have a **credit card** ready ($99/year fee).

## Step 1 — Apple Developer Program enrollment

**This is the first gate.** Read aloud:

> The Apple Developer Program costs **$99/year** (individuals) or
> **$299/year** (organizations — requires a DUNS number which can take
> 2-4 weeks to obtain). Enrollment approval is usually **24-48 hours** for
> individuals, longer for organizations.
>
> If you're an individual developer, you can proceed now. If you need an
> organization account, start the DUNS process first and come back.

Walk through:

1. Open https://developer.apple.com/programs/enroll/
2. Sign in with the Apple ID that will own the account.
3. Choose account type: **Individual** or **Organization**.
4. Pay the $99/year fee.
5. **STOP.** Wait for enrollment confirmation email (usually 24-48h for individuals).

After enrollment is confirmed, the user can access:
- https://developer.apple.com/account — certificates, identifiers, profiles
- https://appstoreconnect.apple.com — app listings, TestFlight, App Store submissions

## Step 2 — Register the App ID

In Apple Developer portal:

1. Open https://developer.apple.com/account/resources/identifiers/list
2. Click "+" to register a new identifier.
3. Choose "App IDs" → "App" (not "App Clip").
4. Description: human-readable name (e.g., "My App - QA").
5. Bundle ID: **Explicit** → enter the exact bundle ID from the Xcode project.
6. Capabilities: enable what you need (Push Notifications, Associated Domains, etc.).
7. Click "Register".

**Repeat for each environment** if you use separate bundle IDs:
- `com.company.appname` (production)
- `com.company.appname-QA` (QA/testing)

## Step 3 — Create signing certificates

### Distribution certificate (for App Store + ad-hoc):

```bash
# Install Fastlane first if not already
gem install fastlane

# Generate distribution certificate
fastlane cert --development false \
  --username "your@appleid.com" \
  --team_id "<your-team-id>"
```

Or via Xcode:
1. Xcode → Settings → Accounts → select your team
2. Click "Manage Certificates..."
3. Click "+" → "Apple Distribution"

Verify:
```bash
security find-identity -v -p codesigning | grep "Apple Distribution"
```

**IMPORTANT:** Each team can only have a limited number of distribution
certificates. If one already exists (from another team member), get the
`.p12` export from them rather than creating a new one. Creating a new one
may revoke theirs.

### Development certificate (for running on physical devices):

```bash
fastlane cert --development true \
  --username "your@appleid.com" \
  --team_id "<your-team-id>"
```

## Step 4 — Create provisioning profiles

For ad-hoc distribution (Firebase App Distribution to testers):
```bash
fastlane sigh --adhoc \
  --app_identifier "com.company.appname-QA" \
  --team_id "<your-team-id>" \
  --force
```

For App Store distribution (TestFlight + App Store):
```bash
fastlane sigh \
  --app_identifier "com.company.appname" \
  --team_id "<your-team-id>" \
  --force
```

For development (running on physical devices):
```bash
fastlane sigh --development \
  --app_identifier "com.company.appname-QA" \
  --team_id "<your-team-id>" \
  --force
```

Verify:
```bash
ls ~/Library/MobileDevice/Provisioning\ Profiles/*.mobileprovision
```

## Step 5 — Firebase project + App Distribution

### 5a — Create Firebase project

1. Open https://console.firebase.google.com
2. Click "Add project" → name it (e.g., `MyApp` or `MyApp-QA`).
3. Google Analytics: optional. Skip if unsure.
4. Wait for creation (~30s).

### 5b — Register the iOS app

1. Project home → click the iOS icon to add an iOS app.
2. **Bundle ID** = exact bundle ID from Xcode (e.g., `com.company.appname-QA`).
3. **App nickname** = cosmetic, anything helpful.
4. Download `GoogleService-Info.plist` when prompted.
5. Add it to the Xcode project (drag into the project, ensure "Copy items if needed" is checked and the correct target is selected).

### 5c — Enable App Distribution

1. Firebase Console → left sidebar → **App Distribution** (under Release & Monitor).
2. Click through the enable flow if prompted.
3. **Testers and groups** tab → **Add group** → create a group (e.g., `qa-testers`).
4. Add at least one tester email.

### 5d — Create the upload service account

For CLI-based uploads (what Fastlane will use):

1. Firebase Console → Project Settings → **Service accounts** tab.
2. Click "Generate new private key."
3. Save the JSON securely. You'll reference it in Fastlane config later.

**Alternative (simpler):** Use a Firebase CLI token instead:
```bash
firebase login:ci
# Copy the printed token — you'll put it in config.yml
```

## Step 6 — Set up Fastlane from scratch

### 6a — Initialize Fastlane

```bash
cd <your-project-root>
fastlane init
```

Choose option 4 ("Manual setup") when prompted — we'll configure lanes manually.

This creates:
- `fastlane/Fastfile` — lane definitions (empty template)
- `fastlane/Appfile` — Apple ID and team info

### 6b — Configure Appfile

Edit `fastlane/Appfile`:
```ruby
for_platform :ios do
  apple_id "your@appleid.com"
  team_name "Your Team Name"
  team_id "<your-team-id>"

  for_lane :qa do
    app_identifier "com.company.appname-QA"
  end

  for_lane :appstore do
    app_identifier "com.company.appname"
  end
end
```

### 6c — Install Firebase plugin

```bash
fastlane add_plugin firebase_app_distribution
```

This creates/updates `fastlane/Pluginfile`.

### 6d — Create config.yml

Create `fastlane/config.yml`:
```yaml
default: &default
  team_id: "<your-team-id>"
  app_name: 'YourApp'
  build_target: 'YourApp'
  scheme_name: 'YourApp'
  app_specific_password: '<generate-at-appleid.apple.com>'
  slack:
    notify: false
    url: ''
    channel: ''
  firebase: &firebase
    cli_token: "<your-firebase-cli-token>"
    test_groups:
      - qa-testers

qa:
  <<: *default
  scheme_name: 'Release - QA'
  firebase:
    <<: *firebase
    app_id: '<firebase-app-id-from-console>'

appstore:
  <<: *default
  scheme_name: 'Release - Production'
  app_identifier: 'com.company.appname'
```

### 6e — Create Fastfile with lanes

Create `fastlane/Fastfile`:
```ruby
platform :ios do
  desc "Distribute QA build to Firebase"
  lane :qa do
    build_ios_app(
      export_method: "ad-hoc",
      scheme: "Release - QA",
      export_team_id: "<your-team-id>",
      xcargs: "-allowProvisioningUpdates"
    )
    firebase_app_distribution(
      app: "<firebase-app-id>",
      groups: "qa-testers",
      firebase_cli_token: "<your-token>",
      release_notes: "QA build"
    )
  end

  desc "Build and upload to TestFlight"
  lane :appstore do
    build_ios_app(
      export_method: "app-store",
      scheme: "Release - Production",
      export_team_id: "<your-team-id>",
      xcargs: "-allowProvisioningUpdates"
    )
    upload_to_testflight(
      skip_waiting_for_build_processing: true
    )
  end
end
```

### 6f — App Store Connect authentication (pick one)

TestFlight uploads authenticate to App Store Connect **separately from code
signing**. Two options, with very different headless behavior:

**Option A — App Store Connect API key (preferred; no 2FA, CI-safe).**
Requires **Account Holder or Admin** on the team to generate.
1. App Store Connect → **Users and Access → Integrations → App Store Connect API**
2. Generate a **Team Key** (App Manager role is enough for TestFlight)
3. Save the three values: **Issuer ID** (UUID), **Key ID** (10 chars), and the
   **`.p8`** (downloadable once) → `fastlane/AuthKey_<KeyID>.p8`, gitignored
4. Wire it ahead of the upload in the Fastfile:
   ```ruby
   app_store_connect_api_key(
     key_id: "XXXXXXXXXX",
     issuer_id: "xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx",
     key_filepath: "./fastlane/AuthKey_XXXXXXXXXX.p8"
   )
   ```
   `upload_to_testflight` then needs no Apple ID, no password, no 2FA.

**Option B — Apple ID + app-specific password + 2FA session (fallback).**
Use this when you can't create an API key (no Admin access). The upload step
becomes **interactive** (a 2FA prompt) unless you pre-seed a session.
1. Generate an app-specific password at https://appleid.apple.com → Security →
   App-Specific Passwords; put it in `config.yml` and export it:
   ```bash
   export FASTLANE_APPLE_APPLICATION_SPECIFIC_PASSWORD="xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx"
   ```
   (That covers the binary upload, but build-management API calls still need a
   2FA web session.)
2. Either run interactively and type the 6-digit code when prompted, **or**
   pre-seed a ~30-day session:
   ```bash
   fastlane spaceauth -u your@appleid.com   # prompts once; prints FASTLANE_SESSION='...'
   ```
   Put the printed `export FASTLANE_SESSION='...'` line into `~/.zshenv` **using
   a text editor** (one physical line). An agent's non-interactive shell sources
   `~/.zshenv`, so it inherits the session and the 2FA prompt is pre-satisfied.
   Re-run `spaceauth` (and re-edit) when it expires (~30 days).
   - **Never write the session via a shell command** (`echo`, and especially
     `pbpaste >> ~/.zshenv`). If the clipboard ever holds the wrong text this
     becomes a self-replicating line that fills `~/.zshenv` and hangs every
     shell. Editor only. Verify with `zsh -c 'echo ${FASTLANE_SESSION:+set}'`.

> **`spaceauth` does NOT skip 2FA, and is NOT a replacement for the
> app-specific password.** It *is* the 2FA step — you do 2FA **once** to mint
> the session, and the cached `FASTLANE_SESSION` then lets later runs skip the
> prompt for ~30 days. The session only covers the App Store Connect web API;
> the binary upload still uses the app-specific password, so Option B needs
> **both**. Net effect: `spaceauth` avoids needing an **API key** and avoids
> **re-entering 2FA every run** — it does not eliminate 2FA itself.

Default to Option A. Only fall back to Option B if API-key creation is blocked
by team-role access.

**Placeholder footgun (read this if you wire your helper to read the key from
config):** make the "use the API key" decision check that the values are
**real** (not `REPLACE_WITH_*`) AND that the `.p8` **exists on disk** — not just
that the keys are present. A presence-only check treats placeholder strings as
"configured," takes the API-key branch, and dies on a missing `.p8` without ever
trying the session. If you're on Path B, the safest config is to **omit the
`app_store_connect_api_key` block entirely**.

**Unattended agent/CI runs — interactive prompts need a real PTY.** If your
Fastfile has *any* interactive prompt (e.g. a `Bump build number? (y/n)`),
**piping the answer does NOT work**: `printf 'y\n' | fastlane …` gives no TTY,
so fastlane's `prompt` returns the empty default (and may crash, e.g.
`undefined method 'downcase' for nil`). Drive it under a pseudo-TTY with
`expect`:
```expect
#!/usr/bin/expect -f
set timeout -1
spawn fastlane ios appstore
expect "(y/n)"
send "y\r"
expect eof
```
With Path A (API key) or a pre-seeded Path B session, auth needs no input, so
`expect` only has to answer the prompt — fully unattended.

## Step 7 — App Store Connect listing

**Gate check:** This creates a public listing. Confirm the user is ready.

1. Open https://appstoreconnect.apple.com
2. My Apps → "+" → New App
3. Fill required fields:
   - Platform: iOS
   - Name: the user-visible app name
   - Primary language
   - Bundle ID: select from dropdown (registered in Step 2)
   - SKU: unique internal identifier (often same as bundle ID reversed)
4. Click "Create"

Before you can submit ANY build for review, Apple requires:
- **Privacy Policy URL** — must be a public URL
- **App screenshots** — at least one set for the required device sizes
- **App description** — at least a short one
- **Category** — primary and optionally secondary
- **Age rating** — questionnaire
- **App Privacy (nutrition labels)** — what data you collect

Tell the user: *"The metadata requirements will block your first TestFlight
external-tester submission. Set aside 30-60 min to fill them out. Internal
TestFlight testing (up to 100 team members) does NOT require this."*

## Step 8 — First TestFlight upload (the unblocker)

The first build uploaded to TestFlight triggers **Apple's Beta App Review**
(24-48 hour wait). After this initial review, subsequent builds are usually
available immediately for internal testers.

```bash
cd <your-project-root> && fastlane ios appstore
```

After upload:
1. Go to App Store Connect → TestFlight tab
2. Wait for "Processing" to complete (~15-30 min)
3. Internal testers (up to 100 team members) can install immediately
4. External testers require submitting for Beta App Review first

**The safety guard:** `upload_to_testflight(skip_waiting_for_build_processing:
true)` uploads but does NOT submit for review or distribute to any group.
A human must:
- Add the build to a tester group (internal or external)
- For external: submit for Beta App Review
- For App Store release: go to the App Store tab and submit for full review

This is the iOS equivalent of Android's `releaseStatus.DRAFT` — the agent
can ship to TestFlight, but cannot release to production without human
intervention.

## Step 9 — Verify the Firebase QA pipeline

```bash
cd <your-project-root> && fastlane ios qa
```

Expected: build succeeds, .ipa uploads to Firebase, testers in `qa-testers`
group receive a notification email within ~1 minute.

If it fails:
- **"No signing certificate found"** → Step 3 wasn't completed
- **"No provisioning profile found"** → Step 4 wasn't completed
- **Firebase upload error** → check `cli_token` and `app_id` in config
- **Build error** → the scheme name in config doesn't match Xcode

## Step 10 — Wire `.cursor/rules/` for the new project

This is what makes the agentic delivery work:

1. Copy `ios-project.mdc` into the new project at `.cursor/rules/ios-project.mdc`
2. Update bundle IDs (replace `com.mdrt.MDRTAcademy*` with your bundle IDs)
3. Update scheme names to match your project's schemes
4. Update fastlane lane references if you named them differently
5. Update the codebase-orientation section for your project structure
6. Remove MDRT-specific gotchas (EightBitVideo, etc.) and add your own

Optionally also copy:
- `ios-project-onboarding.mdc` — update credential paths and tool versions
- `ios-project-bootstrap.mdc` — this file. Keep it so the next person bootstrapping a new project has the playbook.

## Step 11 — Smoke-test the full agentic loop

Open Cursor in the new project. Verify the rule is loaded:
> What do you know about this project?

The agent should recite back the bundle IDs, schemes, and fastlane lanes.

Then test the three-option menu:
> Ship a build to my testers.

The agent should present the 3 options. Pick option 1. Watch it run
`fastlane ios qa`. Confirm the tester email arrives.

If that works end-to-end, **the bootstrap is done.**

## What you've actually built (recap for the user)

After all 11 steps, the new project has:

- An Apple Developer Program account with registered App IDs
- Distribution + development certificates in the local keychain
- Provisioning profiles for ad-hoc (Firebase) and App Store distribution
- A Firebase project with App Distribution + tester groups
- Fastlane configured with `qa` and `appstore` lanes
- An App Store Connect listing (metadata filled, ready for submissions)
- A TestFlight pipeline where upload ≠ release (human-gated safety)
- A project rule the agent reads on every chat so it knows conventions

That's the full delivery surface. From this point on, every release is one
prompt — exactly the P3 claim from the talk.

## Common bootstrap failures

- **"Your account does not have permission"** → Apple Developer enrollment isn't complete. Wait for confirmation email.
- **"No matching provisioning profile"** → the bundle ID in Xcode doesn't match what's registered in Apple Developer portal. They must be identical, case-sensitive.
- **"App ID is not available"** → someone already registered that bundle ID on another team. Choose a different one.
- **TestFlight processing stuck** → builds can take up to 30 min to process. If longer, check App Store Connect for a "Missing Compliance" banner (requires answering the export compliance question).
- **"Missing required icon"** → the Xcode asset catalog doesn't have a 1024x1024 App Store icon. Add it.
- **Firebase upload returns "permission denied"** → CLI token expired. Regenerate with `firebase login:ci`.
- **Beta App Review rejection** → usually metadata issues (missing privacy policy, crashy app). Fix and resubmit — subsequent reviews are faster.
